  "text": "Allergies:\nAmoxicillin\n\nChief Complaint:\n\"I hurt my neck.\"\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nAsked by ER to acutely evaluate pt for question of whether pt\ncould cooperate or be medicated such that she could remain still\nfor MRI without having to be intubated. When I went to see pt,\nshe was in four-point restraints, with her neck in a cervical\ncollar and head restrained to the board (pending c-spine\nclearance). ED staff reported that pt had been very combative,\nwould not lay still, continued to try to sit up and remove\ncollar, would not answer questions or cooperative with exam. \nMom endorsed that pt often becomes very easily agitated at\nbaseline, that her current behavior is not particularly out of\ncharacter. I let ER staff know that pt would need full eval by\npsych prior to d/c; ___ in chart for that purpose. \n\nAdvised ER staff of the following: Does not appear that pt will\ncooperate independently with either cervical collar or for MRI,\nand appears likely that pt will need very heavy sedation to\nremain motionless for study. Do not have any alternative\nmedication recommendations that would enable heavy\nsedation/paralysis without need for airway protection. Will\nreeval patient once she is awake, alert, and cooperative. \n\nOf note, Mom reports that pt had given permission earlier today\n(while coherent) for Mom to receive information about her\ncondition. \n\nOnce acute medical management issues were resolved, ED was also\nwanting psych eval for Mom's report of ongoing SI in her\ndaughter. Pt was laying quietly while collateral information was\nobtained from Mom, but would intermittently yell at the top of\nher lungs \"MOM!\" and would try to disengage from the restraints\nand demanding to be let out of restraints. Speech was slurred, \npt\nwhimpered at times and would yell, but then would fall back\nasleep quickly.  Given that pt clearly could not cooperate with\nexam and was already extremely sedated on the 15mg Haldol, \nAtivan\nand benadryl she received at the OSH, my recommendation to the \nER\nstaff was that \n\nCollateral info from Mom is as follows:\nMom reports that since the age of ___, pt has had wild mood\nswings, manipulates people easily and frequently, and generally\ngets her own way no matter what. Mom states \"Her bark is as bad\nas her bite\" and endorses that pt frequently says very hurtful\nthings, has been physically violent towards her mother (and was\narrested and placed on probation (still on probation currently)\nfor one such incident when pt was ___). Mom also endorses that\npatient has frequently endorses suicidal ideation and often uses\nit as a threat or as a manipulation tool. However, Mom reports\nfeeling extremely concerned re:this issue, and states that ___\nweeks ago, pt called her mother from somewhere in the middle of\nthe woods where she was drinking and using ___ and told her\nmother that she was thinking about killing herself. Mom also\nreports recent escalation in pt's cutting herself on the arms,\nand that pt has started cutting her arms in front of her mother\nbut at times assures her mother that she's not trying to kill\nherself. Also endorses that pt screamed at her mother \"I just\ndon't care anymore\" and then stabbed herself in the arm with a\nballpoint pen. Mother denies past hx of specific suicide\nattempts. Also reports that she believes that her daughter's\nabuse of ___ has been escalating over the last few weeks.\nMom is extremely concerned for the safety of not only her\ndaughter but also herself.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nPsych: When Ms. ___ arrived on the unit, she was quite \nlabile, distractible, and impulsive. She endorsed depressed mood \nrecently, but denied any suicidal ideation and did not remember \nmaking statements to that effect in the ED. She confided in the \nteam that she had been the victim of a recent sexual trauma; she \nfelt that the recent increase in her abuse of alcohol and \nbenzodiazepines was attributable in part to trying to \npsychologically escape this event. She stabilized in the milieu \nand participated in groups regularly. She was started on \nseroquel for her anxiety, insomnia, mood lability, and low \nfrustration tolerance, and this was titrated up during her \nadmission. She consistently denied experiencing excess sedation \nor other side effects, and found this to be beneficical. She was \nalso given small doses of ativan to address her initial anxiety, \nbut she was completely weaned off this medication by the time of \ndischarge. During the admission, her mood brightened and she \ndisplayed less lability. She signed a 3 day notice on the day \nafter admission, and this expired on ___. At that time, the \nteam felt that she was safe to return home, as she was not \nassessed to have any suicidal or homicidal ideation. She was \nlooking forward to get togethers with friends and enrolling in \nschool. Due to limitations in her insurance coverage, she was \nnot able to afford prescription medications upon discharge. The \nteam provided her with a voucher to obtain free Seroquel for one \nmonth's time, while she resolved her insurance difficulties. \nMuch time was spent with the patient and her mother discussing \nlimitations of insurance and options the family could pursue to \noptain more comprehensive prescription coverage. She was \ndischarged home with her mother to follow up with her \nPediatrician the following day. An intake for a therapist was \nalso arranged and she can be referred to a psychiatrist in this \nclinic after establishing care with her therapist. The patient \ndeclined services from the ___ Violence prevention and \nrecovery. \n\nOver the course of the admission, several family meetings were \nheld with the patient and her mother. The patients chronic mood \ninstability, prescription misuse (pain meds, benzos), past \naggressive behavior, and commuinication difficulties were \ndiscussed in detail. The patient and her mother understand the \npatients chronic risk into relapsing into the mood state she \npresented with when under great stress and the importance of \ncomprehensive outpaitent follow up. The patient was in good \nbehavioral control at time of dischagre and her mother is going \nto continue to support her, monitor her safety through time and \nassist in sorting through the insurance problems.\n\nOrthopedics: In the ED, she was diagnosed with a C2 avulsion \nfracture and placed in a ___ collar. She was followed by \nOrthopedics as an inpatient. Her pain was controlled initially \nwith ibuprofen, and later with small doses of tramodol. She was \ntold that she needed to wear the collar at all times until she \ncould be further assessed by Orthopedics in 2 weeks time as an \noutpatient. Detail instructions were provided by orhtopedics \nnurse in terms of the ongoing collar use and follow up.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nI. Mood disorder NOS, R/o MDD, R/o bipolar II, depression;\nAnxiety D/o R/o PTSD \nII. cluster B traits\nIII. C2 avulsion fracture \nIV. family conflict, past legal problems\nV. 40-50",
